<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Espírito Santo</h1><p class="st-meta">1535 CE – 1821 CE · capital Vitória</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Portuguese captaincy on Brazil's southeastern coast founded in 1535, which survived indigenous wars and pirate raids to become a province and state of Brazil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">King John III</a> granted the captaincy of Espírito Santo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-fernandes-coutinho">Vasco Fernandes Coutinho</a>, who landed in 1535 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vila-velha">Vila Velha</a>. Attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goitaca">Goitacá</a> and other peoples forced settlers to move to an island, where the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitoria-espirito-santo">Vitória</a> was founded in 1551 and became the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Espírito Santo grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and was a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions, including that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-anchieta">José de Anchieta</a>, who died there in 1597. The captaincy remained small and poor, raided by French, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> privateers. In the 18th century the crown discouraged roads into the interior to protect the gold district of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The crown bought the captaincy from its proprietors in 1718, and it was placed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> before regaining its own governor in 1810. In 1821 it became a province, and later a state of the Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-espirito-santo.jpg" alt="Captaincy of Espírito Santo" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Floppa Historico · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_do_Brasil_em_1534.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1535 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vitória</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasco Fernandes Coutinho</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Espírito Santo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=captaincy-of-espirito-santo" aria-label="Captaincy of Espírito Santo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3594 2929 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of Espírito Santo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3914 3185L3944 2917"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3914 3185h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3914 3185h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vitória. <a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=captaincy-of-espirito-santo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

